[# Section 5 — analytics, columnar formats, and bridges.
   Authored 2026-05-22; repaired 2026-08-20 against the verification
   audit; re-voiced 2026-09-29 (RULED: DOCS-43, DOCS-47, DOCS-49 wave 6,
   RS-30): the columnar tour was written against one binding's reader —
   twelve examples in a host language — and is now CX's own story: the
   table shape and its cells are corpus cases of the data ring's table
   suite, replayed byte-exact when the page is built; the wire form, the
   Arrow stream and the Parquet adapters are named as the formats and the
   corpus families that grade them, and the bindings are named as
   repositories, never as a language. Anchored in
   spec/03-approved/misc/table-api.md + spec/03-approved/core/data-bin.md
   §3.10-3.13 + spec/03-approved/core/streaming.md of cx-core-data.
   Cross-links use [[anchor]] form. #]

[section id=analytics group='Ring 0 — Data' n=5 title='Analytics, columnar formats, and bridges'

  [child id=overview n=5.1 title='The analytics lane'
    [intro
      """**TL;DR — a table is a CX element, so everything we promise for a
         document holds for a billion rows.** The same brackets that carry a
         configuration file carry a typed columnar table; the same canonical
         bytes and address that anchor one document anchor a dataset; the
         same evaluator that renders a report streams over a file larger
         than memory. The analytics lane has three load-bearing pieces —
         a logical shape, a physical wire form, and bridges to the
         columnar ecosystem — and this section is how they compose. Every
         example on it is a case of the data ring's table suite, replayed
         against this guide's `cx` when the page was built."""]

    [body
      """The three pieces are: a **logical data shape** (the `[table[…]]`
         block, with typed columns and nullable cells); a **physical wire
         form** (CXCol, the column-oriented streaming binary form,
         tag `0x63` in [`spec/core/data-bin.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-data/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/data-bin.md) §3.11);
         and **bridges** (the Arrow C Data Interface for zero-copy handoff
         in process, the Arrow IPC stream for transport, Parquet for
         cross-tool storage)."""]

    [list
      [item """**Logical**: `[orders [table[id::int customer::string]] …]`
              — see [[table-shape]]. Authored in CX text, parsed into the
              same tree as any other element, validated against `.cxs`
              schemas, addressed by SHA-256 over canonical bytes."""]
      [item """**Physical**: CXCol — row-group chunked, optionally
              zstd-compressed per chunk, optionally schema-driven for
              density. See [[cxcol]]."""]
      [item """**Bridges**: the Arrow stream out of CXCol, and Parquet
              through it. See [[arrow-bridge]] and [[parquet-bridge]]."""]
      [item """**Streaming**: the streaming evaluator
              ([`spec/core/streaming.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-data/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/streaming.md))
              emits `StartTable` / `RowGroup` / `EndTable` events over
              CXCol input, so a `[?for]` or a `[?modify]` walks a table
              that does not fit in memory one row group at a time."""]
      [item """**Identity**: a CXCol file has one SHA-256 over its
              canonical (uncompressed) bytes. The compression level does
              not move it; the chunk boundaries do — see
              [[cxcol-identity]]."""]]

    [body
      """The normative documents are
         [`spec/core/data-bin.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-data/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/data-bin.md)
         §3.10-3.13 (the wire form) and
         [`spec/misc/table-api.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-data/blob/main/spec/03-approved/misc/table-api.md)
         (the table surface every binding exposes); this section is the
         working summary."""]

    [note
      """**Naming.** `CXCol` is the column-oriented streaming format. The
         `CXDB` namespace is reserved for a future query engine and does
         not name the file format. `.cxbin` is the file extension
         (`.cxcol` is a deprecated alias, read-only —
         [`spec/process/governance.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx/blob/main/spec/03-approved/process/governance.md)
         §12.2); the `cx_table_*` prefix is the C ABI streaming family
         ([`spec/core/abi.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-data/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/abi.md) §2.10)."""]]

  [child id=table-shape n=5.2 title='The [table[…]] data shape'
    [intro
      """The `[table[…]]` clause-child opener declares a typed columnar
         table at the logical layer. It is a CX element like any other —
         same brackets, same parse rules, same canonical-bytes contract —
         and the parser recognizes the glued `[table[` opener (grammar
         [29]) and produces a table value rather than a generic element.
         The element's own name is the table's name; the round trip through
         the data reading is byte-exact:"""]
    [fixture id=tab-001-typed-columns]

    [body
      """The `[table[…]]` clause carries the column spec: whitespace-
         separated `name::type` entries, where an entry without a type
         declares a string column. The body is a flat run of
         whitespace-separated cells, partitioned into rows by the column
         count (grammar [29c] — each row supplies exactly one cell per
         column). A typed column's cells are typed on read, as any scalar
         is:"""]
    [fixture id=tab-005-typed-int-cells]

    [body
      """A cell that cannot carry its declared column type is refused
         loudly rather than coerced — the encode-refusal rule of
         [`spec/core/data-bin.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-data/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/data-bin.md)
         §3.10.3, never wrap, never widen, never approximate — and so is a
         column whose declared type and cell annotation disagree (the table
         suite's `tab-026` pins that refusal; its recorded diagnostic is a
         message rather than a code, which is why this page names it
         instead of replaying it)."""]

    [body
      """Supported column types match the scalar type system:
         `int` and the sized integers (`i8`–`i64`, `u8`–`u64`),
         `bigint`, `float` and the reduced widths (`f32`, `f16`),
         `decimal`, `bool`, `string`, `bytes`, `date`, and `datetime`.
         The full catalog with byte encodings is in
         [`spec/core/data-bin.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-data/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/data-bin.md)
         §3.10.3 + [`spec/misc/type-mapping.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-data/blob/main/spec/03-approved/misc/type-mapping.md)."""]

    [child id=nullable n=5.2.1 title='Null cells'
      [body
        """Any column can carry null cells. A null cell is written as the
           literal `null` — a bare token in cell position; an empty cell is
           not allowed in the wire form:"""]
      [fixture id=tab-009-null-cell]
      [body
        """On the wire, a column with null cells uses the nullable wrapper
           of [`spec/core/data-bin.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-data/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/data-bin.md)
           §3.10.5: type code `0x80`, then the inner type code, then a null
           bitmap of `ceil(rows/8)` bytes (bit set = null), then the packed
           payload of the non-null cells only. Each binding's table surface
           answers a null cell with its host's own "no value" form."""]]

    [child id=collection-cells n=5.2.2 title='Collection cells'
      [body
        """A cell is a value, so a cell may be a collection — an array or
           a map literal — and the table stays a table:"""]
      [fixture id=tab-012-array-cells]
      [fixture id=tab-013-map-cells]
      [body
        """A table may also nest inside an element like any child, and an
           element that happens to be named `table` composes as an ordinary
           element — only the glued opener declares a table:"""]
      [fixture id=tab-027-nested-one-deep]]

    [child id=dict-encoding n=5.2.3 title='Dictionary encoding'
      [body
        """Dictionary encoding is an **opt-in container form** — tag
           `0x62` in [`spec/core/data-bin.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-data/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/data-bin.md)
           §3.10.2. Each column's payload starts with a marker: plain
           (the §3.10.3 layout) or dictionary-encoded (distinct values
           in insertion order, then one varint index per row). Decoders
           rebuild the values transparently; no table surface exposes the
           indices."""]
      [body
        """Canonical writers emit the plain `0x60` form. A writer that opts
           into `0x62` uses a dictionary for a column only when the
           dictionary actually saves bytes — the canonical constraint of
           §3.10.2, evaluated per column — so categorical data (status,
           region, type) dictionary-encodes and high-cardinality data stays
           plain. CXPath `=` and `[?match]` compare the decoded value, so a
           program is dictionary-agnostic."""]]

    [child id=table-validate n=5.2.4 title='Schema validation'
      [body
        """Tables validate against `.cxs` schemas the way ordinary elements
           do — the table block is an element, and the schema language of
           [`spec/core/schema.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-data/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/schema.md)
           applies to it as to any other. See [[data#schema]] for the
           language. At the binary layer, a CXCol file can carry a schema
           reference that names its schema by content hash (see
           [[cxcol-schema-driven]])."""]]

    [child id=table-api n=5.2.5 title='The table surface of a binding'
      [intro
        """Every binding exposes a table value for `[table[…]]` blocks —
           one surface, ratified in
           [`spec/misc/table-api.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-data/blob/main/spec/03-approved/misc/table-api.md)
           §3, spelled in each host's own naming: the `cols` / `types` /
           `row_count` / `col_count` properties; the `row` / `column` /
           `col_at` / `cell` / `cell_by_name` accessors; the `slice` /
           `head` / `tail` / `select_cols` projections; row iteration; and
           the `to_cx` / `to_csv` / `to_json` / `to_data_bin` /
           `to_dict_list` conversions. The four shipping bindings are on
           [[bindings]]; what a table method
           answers is what the corpus cases above answer, because the
           surface is a projection of the same value."""]]]

  [child id=cxcol n=5.3 title='CXCol — column-oriented streaming format'
    [intro
      """CXCol is the physical wire form for tabular data: column-oriented,
         row-group-chunked, content-addressable, and streamable in bounded
         memory. The data-bin tag is `0x63`; the file extension is
         `.cxbin`. The normative byte-level layout is in
         [`spec/core/data-bin.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-data/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/data-bin.md) §3.11,
         and the data ring's corpus grades it in four families —
         `data_bin_chunked`, `data_bin_compression`,
         `data_bin_schema_driven` and `data_bin_arrow` — each case a CX
         document beside the exact bytes it must encode to. Those cases
         record bytes, not text, so this page names them rather than
         replaying them."""]

    [body
      """The format is a single sequential pass: header, column spec, then
         a stream of row groups, then a terminator. A writer emits one row
         group at a time and produces a billion-row table in memory bounded
         by the largest row group; a reader pulls row groups one at a time
         and skips a group by its length prefix without decoding it."""]

    [child id=cxcol-layout n=5.3.1 title='Wire layout'
      [body
        """A CXCol stream is:"""]
      [list
        [item """**12-byte header.** Magic `CXCol` (5 bytes,
                `0x43 0x58 0x43 0x6F 0x6C`), version byte (`0x01`),
                flags byte, `max_depth` (uint32 LE), reserved byte.
                Flag bit 0 is endianness (1 = little-endian); flag
                bit 1 signals schema-driven mode (see
                [[cxcol-schema-driven]]); bits 2–7 are reserved and
                must be zero."""]
        [item """**Column spec.** The chunked-table tag `0x63`, then
                `uvarint(col_count)`, then one col-spec per column —
                the column name as a tagged string plus its type code
                (the §3.10.1 shape). Declared once at the head — every
                row group in the file conforms to this spec."""]
        [item """**Row-group sequence.** Each group is
                `uvarint(body_byte_len) <body-tag> <body>`. `body-tag`
                `0x01` = plain (`uvarint(row_count)` then column-major
                buffers); `0x90` = the zstd page-compression wrapper
                (§3.12), whose decompressed bytes form the same plain
                body."""]
        [item """**Terminator.** A single `0x00` byte (a zero-length
                row group) closes the stream."""]]
      [body
        """2²⁰ (1,048,576) rows per group is the canonical chunk size —
           `cx canonical` and `cx hash` re-chunk a CXCol input to it, and a
           writer that wants cross-system hash invariance uses it. A
           different chunk size produces a valid CXCol file with a different
           SHA-256."""]]

    [child id=cxcol-identity n=5.3.2 title='Content-addressable identity'
      [body
        """A CXCol file's SHA-256 is computed over its **canonical
           uncompressed bytes** — the bytes you would see if every `0x90`
           page-compression wrapper were unwrapped to its `0x01` plain
           form. Compression is a transport and storage choice; identity is
           a content choice; the format keeps them orthogonal, and the
           `data_bin_compression` cases pin that a compressed and a plain
           encoding of one table share one address."""]
      [list
        [item """**Same data + same chunking + same schema → same hash**,
                whatever the zstd level, and whether compression was
                applied at all."""]
        [item """**Different chunk size → different hash.** Chunk
                boundaries are part of the canonical form; the
                2²⁰-rows-per-group convention is the cross-writer rule."""]
        [item """**The hash composes with [[hash]].** The same SHA-256
                primitive that addresses a CX document addresses a CXCol
                file — caching, dedup, signing and content-addressed
                storage treat both the same."""]]]

    [child id=cxcol-streaming-api n=5.3.3 title='Streaming reader / writer'
      [intro
        """The streaming surface is the C ABI reader / writer family in
           [`spec/core/abi.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-data/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/abi.md) §2.10
           (capability bit 21): `cx_table_reader_open` /
           `cx_table_reader_open_fd` / `cx_table_reader_schema` /
           `cx_table_reader_next` / `cx_table_reader_close`, and
           `cx_table_writer_open` / `cx_table_writer_open_fd` /
           `cx_table_writer_emit_row_group` /
           `cx_table_writer_close_get_bytes` / `cx_table_writer_close`.
           A reader pulls one row group at a time; a writer pushes one at a
           time and closes to the framed bytes. Each binding wraps the
           family in its host's idiom — a context-managed reader and
           writer where the host has them, the raw symbols where it does
           not — and [[bindings]] says which binding ships which."""]
      [body
        """The memory budget is one row group in flight per stage. A reader
           chained into a CXPath filter chained into a Parquet writer holds
           three row groups at once, whatever the table's logical row
           count. See [[streaming]] for the directive-level view."""]]

    [child id=cxcol-schema-driven n=5.3.4 title='Schema-driven mode'
      [body
        """When both sides of the wire hold the schema, header flag bit 1
           enables schema-driven mode: per-value type tags are omitted for
           the values the schema declares, and reader and writer walk the
           schema in lockstep with the data. Wire density approaches
           Parquet's typed encoding; the `data_bin_schema_driven` cases pin
           the bytes, and a closed-mode schema refuses a column it did not
           declare."""]
      [body
        """Schema-driven mode is opt-in — `cx_to_data_bin --schema-driven`
           at the ABI, the corresponding call in each binding — and a
           schema in scope never silently promotes the encoder. The schema
           reference forms (`0x10` content-hash, `0x11` inline, `0x12`
           content-hash + name hint, `0x13` self-describing multihash) are
           [`spec/core/data-bin.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-data/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/data-bin.md) §3.13.1;
           the `cx table` subcommand resolves `--schema-from=` to a content
           hash before it emits."""]
      [note
        """Schema-driven mode needs the reader to retrieve the schema by
           its content hash. Local lookup is the default; the
           callback-shaped content-store ABI is a planned follow-up."""]]]

  [child id=arrow-bridge n=5.4 title='Arrow bridge — zero-copy interop'
    [intro
      """CXCol bridges to Apache Arrow through the Arrow **C Data
         Interface** — a struct-based ABI under which two libraries share
         columnar buffers without copying them. The CX side is
         `libcx_arrow`, a separate dynamic library over libcx and the Arrow
         C-Data ABI; core libcx is Arrow-free, and capability bit 23
         advertises Arrow when `libcx_arrow` is loaded. The
         `data_bin_arrow` cases of the data ring pin the export and import
         byte for byte."""]

    [body
      """The bridge exports four C ABI symbols
         ([`spec/core/abi.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-data/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/abi.md) §2.11):
         `cx_arrow_export_open` / `cx_arrow_export_open_fd` for
         CXCol → Arrow, and `cx_arrow_import_to_data_bin` /
         `cx_arrow_import_to_data_bin_fd` for Arrow → CXCol. A binding
         wraps them as an export (framed CXCol bytes in, an Arrow record
         batch reader out) and an import (the inverse) — the per-binding
         Arrow libraries are tabulated in §2.11."""]

    [child id=arrow-zero-copy n=5.4.1 title='Zero-copy handoff'
      [body
        """The C Data Interface uses two structs: `ArrowSchema` (column
           types and names) and `ArrowArray` (per-batch buffers — null
           bitmap, offsets, values). CX points these at its own column
           buffers; an Arrow-native consumer reads from the same memory
           pages CX wrote."""]
      [body
        """Ownership crosses the boundary explicitly. The exporter fills an
           `ArrowArrayStream` with a `release` callback; the consumer calls
           `release` when it is done; CX's callback frees the underlying
           CXCol buffers. There is no garbage-collector interplay — a
           binding holds the stream as an opaque handle until it is
           finished with it."""]]

    [child id=arrow-type-mapping n=5.4.2 title='Type mapping'
      [body
        """The CX ↔ Arrow type map is normative
           ([`spec/misc/type-mapping.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-data/blob/main/spec/03-approved/misc/type-mapping.md)).
           In summary:"""]
      [list
        [item """`int` / `i64` ↔ `Int64`; `i32` ↔ `Int32`; the sized
                variants (`i8`–`u64`) map to the corresponding Arrow
                sized integer types."""]
        [item """`float` / `f64` ↔ `Float64`; `f32` ↔ `Float32`;
                `f16` ↔ `Float16`."""]
        [item """`bool` ↔ `Boolean` (bit-packed in Arrow)."""]
        [item """`string` ↔ `Utf8` (or `LargeUtf8` for a column whose
                total byte length exceeds 2 GB)."""]
        [item """`bytes` ↔ `Binary` (or `LargeBinary` similarly)."""]
        [item """`date` ↔ `Date32` (days since 1970-01-01, proleptic
                Gregorian)."""]
        [item """`datetime` ↔ `Timestamp(Nanoseconds, UTC)` (Arrow
                format string `tsn:UTC`)."""]
        [item """`decimal` ↔ `Decimal128` / `Decimal256` by coefficient
                width."""]
        [item """A nullable column → Arrow's nullable variant (validity
                bitmap)."""]]
      [body
        """Column-type coverage in `libcx_arrow` is **all ten column
           types** — int / i8 / i16 / i32 / float / bool / string / date /
           bytes / datetime (the last as `timestamp[ns, UTC]`). Deferred:
           `decimal` and dictionary-encoded columns — each answers a clear
           `not yet supported` error rather than a lossy fallback."""]]

    [child id=arrow-ipc n=5.4.3 title='Arrow IPC stream format'
      [body
        """Beyond in-process handoff, CXCol emits Arrow IPC streams for
           cross-process or cross-host transport. The IPC stream is Arrow's
           own serialization (schema header + record batches + EOF),
           distinct from the C Data Interface (in-memory structs). The
           `cx table dump FILE --to=arrow [--output=FILE]` subcommand
           ([`spec/misc/cli.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-code/blob/main/spec/03-approved/misc/cli.md))
           writes it, and each binding's Arrow module reads and writes it
           as bytes or as a file."""]]

    [child id=arrow-flight n=5.4.4 title='Arrow Flight (planned)'
      [body
        """Arrow Flight is Arrow's gRPC-based RPC framing for cross-process
           record batches — the natural next layer above the IPC stream: a
           Flight server publishes batches by descriptor; a client
           subscribes. CX integration is **planned**: no Flight surface
           ships, and the tooling-layer story will be specified when it
           merges."""]
      [note
        """For cross-process CXCol transport today, carry Arrow IPC over
           your existing transport (HTTP, a pipe, a socket); the
           `[?http-client]` directive carries an IPC stream as a binary
           body."""]]]

  [child id=parquet-bridge n=5.5 title='Parquet bridge'
    [intro
      """CXCol bridges to Apache Parquet through Arrow: CXCol → Arrow stream
         → Parquet file. Core libcx has **no Parquet C++ dependency** —
         adding one would couple every binding to a heavyweight build-time
         toolchain — so the Parquet adapter lives at the binding layer,
         chained off the Arrow bridge.
         [`spec/misc/table-api.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-data/blob/main/spec/03-approved/misc/table-api.md)
         §3 declares the per-binding adapter slot; the PyPI binding ships
         the packaged one-call adapter, and the other bindings reach
         Parquet through their host's Arrow library today."""]

    [child id=parquet-write n=5.5.1 title='Writing Parquet'
      [body
        """The adapter walks framed CXCol bytes → Arrow record batches →
           the host's Parquet writer; the writer is the host's choice, and
           the CXCol side provides the Arrow stream. Defaults are the
           Parquet ecosystem's (Snappy, not zstd), because the output is
           for Parquet readers that assume Snappy; zstd is CXCol's own
           default for its `0x90` page compression. Match the row-group
           size to CXCol's canonical chunk (2²⁰ rows) and the two formats
           chunk alike."""]]

    [child id=parquet-read n=5.5.2 title='Reading Parquet'
      [body
        """The inverse: the host's Parquet reader produces Arrow record
           batches, and the bridge imports them as framed CXCol chunked-table
           bytes — ready for a whole-value read or a streaming reader pass.
           Column projection and predicate pushdown are the **host Parquet
           reader's** work, done before the batches reach CX: a reader given
           per-row-group statistics skips the groups a predicate rules out,
           so the I/O saved is proportional to the groups skipped; a file
           without statistics still reads correctly as a full scan."""]]

    [child id=parquet-types n=5.5.3 title='CX ↔ Parquet type mapping'
      [body
        """The mapping is normative. Most types round-trip cleanly through
           Arrow; a few CX types have lossy edges, and the bridge reports
           them loudly rather than coercing silently."""]
      [list
        [item """`int` / `i64` → `INT64`."""]
        [item """`i32` → `INT32`; `i16` / `i8` → `INT32` with
                `INT(N, true)` logical type; unsigned similarly."""]
        [item """`f64` / `float` → `DOUBLE`; `f32` → `FLOAT`;
                `f16` → `FIXED_LEN_BYTE_ARRAY(2)` / `FLOAT16`
                (the latter non-standard before Parquet 1.13)."""]
        [item """`bool` → `BOOLEAN`."""]
        [item """`string` → `BYTE_ARRAY` with the `STRING` logical type
                (UTF-8)."""]
        [item """`bytes` → `BYTE_ARRAY`."""]
        [item """`date` → `INT32` with the `DATE` logical type."""]
        [item """`datetime` (UTC) → `INT64` with
                `TIMESTAMP(NANOS, isAdjustedToUTC=true)`."""]
        [item """`decimal` → `DECIMAL(p, s)` (Parquet caps precision at
                256 bits)."""]
        [item """A nullable column → `OPTIONAL` repetition."""]]
      [body
        """A type outside the bridge's coverage (see
           [[arrow-type-mapping]]) refuses with a clear error at the Arrow
           boundary rather than degrading — the same loud-never-lossy
           posture as the wire form itself."""]]

    [child id=parquet-roundtrip n=5.5.4 title='Round-trip identity'
      [body
        """CX → Parquet → CX is not in general byte-identical. Parquet is a
           presentation-layer format: footer offsets shift, row-group sizes
           are writer-dependent, encoding choices (dictionary thresholds,
           page sizes, the codec) are per writer; many byte-level Parquet
           files encode the same logical data. CXCol fills exactly that
           niche — the hashable origin; Parquet is the bridge."""]
      [body
        """**Logical** round trip is normative: a CX table emitted as
           Parquet and re-imported produces the same `[table[…]]` block
           with the same column names, types, row count and cell values
           (modulo the documented lossy edges of 5.5.3) — which is the
           byte-exact CX round trip the table suite grades, taken through
           one more format."""]]

    [child id=parquet-metadata n=5.5.5 title='Schema identity across the bridge'
      [body
        """Parquet output carries the data, not the CX schema reference.
           Schema identity lives at the CXCol layer: a schema-driven file
           names its schema by content hash (the §3.13.1 reference forms),
           and `cx table` resolves `--schema-from=` to a content hash before
           it emits
           ([`spec/core/data-bin.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-data/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/data-bin.md)
           §3.13.1). A Parquet round trip re-enters CX as a self-describing
           table; re-attach the schema by validating, or by re-encoding
           schema-driven against the same schema hash."""]]]

  [child id=streaming n=5.6 title='Streaming evaluator over columnar data'
    [intro
      """The streaming evaluator
         ([`spec/core/streaming.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-data/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/streaming.md))
         walks CXCol chunks one at a time. The normative throughput gate is
         **≥ 200 MB/s sustained per streaming shape**
         ([`spec/core/code.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-code/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/code.md)
         §11.4.4, gate 15), re-measured by `make bench-code-streaming`; the
         gate register carries its last honest verdict, and we state it
         there rather than in prose that goes stale."""]

    [body
      """The evaluator emits three table-specific events when it meets a
         CXCol input: **StartTable** (the column spec), **RowGroup** (one
         chunk's data), **EndTable** (close). A directive that consumes the
         stream sees one row group at a time and never materializes the
         whole table."""]

    [body
      """The consumer surface for a CXCol stream today is the CLI and the
         table surface of each binding (`cx table info` / `dump` / `load`,
         and a binding's table methods):"""]

    [example lang=shell
      """cx table info big.cxbin          [# column spec, row count, size #]
         cx table dump big.cxbin --to=cx  [# stream to CX text            #]"""]

    [body
      """The design direction, not yet shipped as a CX-code surface: any
         directive that takes a CXPath iterator participates in a CXCol
         stream — `[?for]` / `[?modify]` / `[?map]` / `[?reduce]` (see
         [[code]]). Bounded memory holds end to end: a pipeline of N stages
         holds N row groups in flight, not N × the row count."""]

    [child id=streaming-events n=5.6.1 title='Streaming events'
      [body
        """The event vocabulary
           ([`spec/core/streaming.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-data/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/streaming.md))
           extends the standard `StartElement` / `Scalar` / `EndElement`
           events with three table-specific variants."""]
      [list
        [item """**StartTable** — fields: `name` (string), `col_spec`
                (binary blob; the column schema in ast_bin form). Emitted
                in place of the `StartElement` that would otherwise wrap
                a `[table[…]]` body."""]
        [item """**RowGroup** — fields: `row_count` (u32), `payload`
                (binary blob; one row group's column buffers). One event
                per row group, in source order."""]
        [item """**EndTable** — fields: `name` (string). Closes the
                sequence in place of the corresponding `EndElement`."""]]
      [body
        """A non-chunked `0x60` (whole-table) input emits the existing
           `StartElement` / per-cell-`Scalar` / `EndElement` sequence
           unchanged; only a `0x63` chunked input produces the new events.
           A consumer that does not care flattens each `RowGroup` into
           `StartElement` / row / `EndElement` triples with a helper."""]]

    [child id=streaming-perf n=5.6.2 title='Performance posture'
      [body
        """The gate protocol is fixed by
           [`spec/core/code.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-code/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/code.md)
           §11.4.4: every measured streaming shape must sustain a mean
           ≥ 200 MB/s with no single trial below 80 % of the mean, and must
           demonstrably stream (more than one output chunk, the
           implementation's own streaming predicate reporting true). Re-run
           with `make bench-code-streaming`; the register row is the
           verdict."""]
      [note
        """On the write side, the ABI streaming writer compresses a row
           group only when its body exceeds 64 KiB and compression beats
           0.85 of the original size
           ([`spec/core/abi.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-data/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/abi.md) §2.10)."""]]]

  [child id=integrations n=5.7 title='Ecosystem integrations'
    [intro
      """The Arrow C Data Interface is the integration layer the analytical
         ecosystem standardized on. Once CX exposes Arrow, the rest of the
         stack lights up — DuckDB, Polars, DataFusion, pandas, Spark by way
         of Parquet — most with zero-copy handoff in process, the rest with
         one copy at the boundary. Each integration below is the Arrow
         bridge of [[arrow-bridge]] plus the consumer's own Arrow entry
         point; the binding you use supplies the glue in its host's idiom,
         and [[bindings]] says which binding packages which."""]

    [child id=duckdb n=5.7.1 title='DuckDB'
      [body
        """DuckDB consumes an Arrow table registered on a connection —
           zero-copy from CXCol's column buffers into DuckDB's vectorized
           executor through the C Data Interface. Export the framed CXCol
           bytes, register the Arrow table, run SQL; DuckDB's executor
           reads CX's column buffers during the scan, the result set is
           materialized in DuckDB's own memory, and the bridge's import
           brings a result back as framed CXCol bytes — the symmetric
           inverse."""]]

    [child id=polars n=5.7.2 title='Polars'
      [body
        """Polars is Arrow-native at the buffer level: CX → Polars goes
           through the same export call — zero-copy for the underlying
           buffers — and a Polars query runs on the frame from there. In
           the Rust binding the same handoff runs over the C ABI: the
           `cx_arrow_export_open` stream feeds the `arrow` crate's
           `ffi_stream` reader, and Polars consumes the record batches; a
           packaged convenience wrapper there has not shipped."""]]

    [child id=pandas n=5.7.3 title='pandas'
      [body
        """pandas is **not** zero-copy at the buffer level — its column
           model is NumPy arrays with type-system mismatches against Arrow
           (no nullable integers, no first-class strings without
           `StringDtype`). CX → pandas is one Arrow round trip plus one
           pandas conversion: expect one full data copy at the boundary.
           [`spec/misc/table-api.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-data/blob/main/spec/03-approved/misc/table-api.md)
           §3 declares a `to_pandas()` adapter slot on the table type that
           no binding has implemented yet; the explicit Arrow route is the
           shipped path."""]
      [note
        """Where zero copy matters, prefer Polars over pandas. pandas stays
           supported because the data-science ecosystem assumes it, but the
           cross-boundary cost is real on large tables."""]]

    [child id=datafusion n=5.7.4 title='DataFusion (planned)'
      [body
        """Apache DataFusion is a Rust query engine over Arrow. Its
           `TableProvider` trait accepts arbitrary Arrow record-batch
           sources, so CXCol is a natural fit: register a `.cxbin` file as
           a DataFusion table and run SQL or DataFrame queries with the
           same zero-copy guarantees as Polars."""]
      [body
        """Status: **planned** — no packaged CX-side convenience layer
           ships. An adopter who needs DataFusion today wires it by hand:
           `cx_arrow_export_open` over the C ABI into the `arrow` crate's
           `ffi_stream` reader, and the record batches into a DataFusion
           `MemTable`. The path works; it lacks the convenience layer."""]]

    [child id=spark n=5.7.5 title='Spark and cluster compute'
      [body
        """Spark reads and writes Parquet natively, so CXCol's Parquet
           bridge (see [[parquet-bridge]]) is the integration path: emit
           `.parquet` from CX, point Spark at the directory, run Spark SQL.
           For zero copy in cluster contexts, Arrow Flight is the long-term
           path; status is **planned** per [[arrow-flight]]."""]
      [body
        """The Parquet bridge is one-way at this layer — Spark's writes
           come back through `cx table load output.parquet --to=cx`. For a
           round-trip workflow, keep the CXCol file as the canonical origin
           and treat Spark's outputs as derived artifacts."""]]]

  [child id=performance n=5.8 title='Performance characteristics'
    [intro
      """The analytics lane's budgets and where each is measured. The spec
         fixes the gates; the bench targets re-measure them; the numbers
         live where they are re-run, not in prose that goes stale."""]

    [list
      [item """**Streaming throughput.** Spec gate: mean ≥ 200 MB/s per
              streaming shape, no trial below 80 % of the mean
              ([`spec/core/code.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-code/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/code.md)
              §11.4.4, gate 15; bench: `make bench-code-streaming`); the
              gate register carries the last honest verdict."""]
      [item """**Parquet write (default Snappy, 2²⁰ row group).** Measured
              by the host's Parquet writer on the CXCol → Arrow → Parquet
              path; throughput is dominated by the Parquet codec, not the
              CXCol bridge. Profile against your chosen writer; no
              normative CX-side gate."""]
      [item """**Arrow zero-copy handoff.** O(metadata) per batch — the
              schema and the per-column buffer pointers are populated, no
              buffer is copied. Time per export is independent of row
              count and proportional to column count."""]
      [item """**Predicate pushdown.** When the Parquet writer emitted
              per-row-group statistics, the host Parquet reader skips the
              groups a predicate rules out — I/O saved is proportional to
              the groups skipped; a file without statistics degrades to a
              full scan."""]
      [item """**Memory budget per stream stage.** One row group in flight.
              The streaming evaluator
              ([`spec/core/streaming.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-data/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/streaming.md))
              is bounded by `max(row_group_byte_size)` whatever the table
              size."""]]

    [note
      """The 200 MB/s gate is for the **streaming evaluator**, not the raw
         CXCol reader — the reader has no evaluator pipeline in front of
         it, and its throughput is not separately gated or published. Per
         binding, adapter throughput varies by host; the pandas route is
         the slowest because of its boundary copy."""]]

  [child id=cxcol-vs-others n=5.9 title='When to use CXCol vs Parquet vs Arrow vs JSON'
    [intro
      """Pick by use case. The four formats overlap in the analytics lane
         and each has a niche the others fill badly."""]

    [list
      [item """**CXCol** — when content-addressable identity matters
              (caching, dedup, signing, provenance) or when you want
              streaming with the CX type system end to end. Single-writer,
              sequential-decode, hashable. A single-file format, not a
              query engine (take the Arrow bridge into DuckDB / Polars /
              DataFusion for that)."""]
      [item """**Parquet** — for cross-ecosystem interop (Spark, BigQuery,
              Snowflake, Trino, Athena, the Hadoop-lineage tools). Carries
              the data, not CX's schema identity or metadata (see
              [[parquet-metadata]]). Not hashable in the byte-stable sense
              — different writers produce different bytes for the same
              logical data."""]
      [item """**Arrow IPC stream** — for in-process or LAN-local zero copy
              between CX and another Arrow-native consumer. No compression
              by default (Arrow is layout-oriented); not designed for
              long-term storage."""]
      [item """**JSON / JSONL** — for ad-hoc human-readable interop or a
              consumer that truly is JSON-shaped. Lossy on type metadata
              (int and float kinds merge, date-times become strings,
              dictionary encoding is gone). The CX → JSON projection (see
              [[surfaces#json]]) handles the shape; for tabular data, JSONL
              (one row per line) is the conventional wire form."""]]

    [body
      """The decision tree, compressed:"""]

    [list
      [item """Need a hashable, content-addressable origin? → **CXCol**."""]
      [item """Sharing with Spark / Snowflake / BigQuery / other
              non-Arrow-native tools? → **Parquet**."""]
      [item """In-process handoff to DuckDB / Polars / DataFusion / pandas?
              → **Arrow** (with CXCol as the origin)."""]
      [item """Debugging, ad-hoc inspection, or a JSON-shaped consumer? →
              **JSON / JSONL**."""]]

    [body
      """In practice the layering is: **CXCol as canonical origin → Arrow
         as the in-process bridge → Parquet for cross-ecosystem export**.
         JSON is the human-readable view. The same logical table moves
         through all four; identity stays at CXCol."""]]

  [child id=see-also n=5.10 title='See also'
    [list
      [item """[[identity]] — canonical bytes + SHA-256. CXCol identity
              rests on the same primitive over canonical bytes; the rule
              extends to chunked tables and schema-driven encoding."""]
      [item """[[surfaces]] — the JSON / YAML / TOML / XML / Markdown
              projections. CXCol is the analytical surface; the others are
              the interchange surfaces."""]
      [item """[[bindings]] — the four shipping bindings, each with the
              table surface and the streaming reader / writer in its
              host's idiom."""]
      [item """[[code#streaming]] — the directive-level streaming view:
              `[?for]` / `[?modify]` over CXCol inputs in bounded
              memory."""]
      [item """[`spec/misc/table-api.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-data/blob/main/spec/03-approved/misc/table-api.md) — the
              typed columnar table surface ratified across the four
              bindings."""]
      [item """[`spec/core/data-bin.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-data/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/data-bin.md) §3.10-3.13 —
              chunked-table wire format, page-compression wrapper,
              schema-driven mode."""]
      [item """[`spec/core/streaming.md`](https://github.com/cx-home/cx-core-data/blob/main/spec/03-approved/core/streaming.md) — the
              streaming evaluator and the StartTable / RowGroup / EndTable
              event vocabulary."""]]]]

[# End of section 5 — analytics #]
